# netsurf: a textarea's rows is its natural height, and a tap on its border places the caret (patch 479)
# Generated 2026-11-27
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 478)
#
# P2 (input on JS-heavy pages): device run 43 -- seven taps on google.com's
# search box gave no focus and no keyboard. Two engine faults, one field:
#
# 1. css/hints.c mapped <textarea rows=N> to an AUTHOR height of N em.
#    Google's field (rows=1, line-height 24px, transparent 12px top and
#    bottom borders, box-sizing: border-box) then had border-box take the
#    borders out of 16px: no content height, a 24px field where browsers
#    draw 48px (Chromium measured: 48 with or without border-box; rows=3
#    96). A tap on its lower half landed on the wrapper DIV. Browsers treat
#    rows as the textarea's NATURAL height -- rows x line-height, default
#    2 -- which box-sizing never touches and any author height overrides.
#    The hint is gone; layout gives an auto-height textarea that height
#    (vb_textarea_natural_height: layout_block_context and the
#    float/inline-block path, which used a fixed 4em).
#
# 2. textarea_mouse_action drops a point past its visible text area, so a
#    tap on the field's bottom or right border (inside the box the engine
#    hit) placed no caret: html_mouse_action now clamps a press or click
#    into the text area, as a browser focuses a field tapped on its border.
#
# The interaction pass gains IN-searchfield.html (Google's field, as its
# CSS builds it) and a tap on its bottom border that must place the caret
# (C_EDITING), then typed text. cols keeps its width hint (same flaw for
# widths, not hit here).
